      <title>Cloudlets: universal server images for the cloud</title>
      <subtitle></subtitle>
      <track>Lightning Talks</track>
      <type></type>
      <language></language>
      <abstract>&#xA;&lt;p&gt;In this talk we will discuss the issue of server images, and how it affects inter-cloud portability. We will describe our vision for a universal format which can be shared and improved as easily as a Git repository, and how we&amp;#39;re implementing it with cloudlets.&#xA;&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;  </abstract>
      <description>&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Cloudlets are universal server images for the cloud. They&amp;#39;re lightweight, version-controlled, and you can export them to any bootable format known to man: Xen, KVM, Amazon EC2, or just a plain bootable CD. They can be shared and distributed with the semantics of tools such as Git and Mercurial.&#xA;&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Our goal is to build the foundations for truly cloud-independent infrastructures. Our roadmap includes:&#xA;&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;  * Multi-image stacks&#xA;  * Auto-scaling&#xA;  * Automated integration tests&#xA;  * In-place image editing&#xA;  * Integration with existing VM generation and configuration management tools&#xA;&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;  </description>
      <persons>
          <person id="331">Solomon Hykes</person>
